WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.899
Film Courage: Jim, I know you joke about food,
you’ve written about food.

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:07.379
Do you ever think about the backstory of what
your character you are about to play eats?

00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:13.640
Jim Gaffigan, Actor, Comic, Author: …That’s
a really interesting question.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.900
I suppose I haven’t.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:28.470
I do think…when we were shooting CHAPPAQUIDDICK
there was an amount of…these scenarios where

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.830
there is drinking, but I think that’s not
food.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:38.550
But like there is this approach to drinking
and consumption of alcohol that has shifted

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.910
so much with every decade (at least in my
lifetime).

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:53.260
So this was kind of my Dad’s era where people
would drink just enormous amounts and they

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.420
would day drink and drink at night (it was
pretty amazing).

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.250
Film Courage: Did you ever consider what your
character might eat?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.970
I know that drinking was a lot of what was
going on especially on those few days that

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.070
the film takes place?

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.670
Jim Gaffigan: I think I…what they would
eat?

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.330
Yeah, I don’t know.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.330
That’s interesting.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.300
I think it’s more the priority of eating.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:27.600
Whereas, I think for me what I would eat and
the type of food I would eat is probably more

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.190
important than what it was back then.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.950
It’s like you look back what we were having
at barbecues as a kid versus versus what we’re

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.470
having at barbecues now.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.010
It’s kind of gotten fancier.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.680
Film Courage: Fondues and things like that.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.680
Jim Gaffigan: Yeah.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.560
Film Courage: Why do you think comedians make
such great actors?

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.590
Jim Gaffigan: Well…I mean that’s flattering.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:02:01.930
I would say that comedians, it’s really
necessary to know yourself, that that’s

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.400
part of the journey and that’s part of the
conversation you have with the audience and

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.890
how they respond to things, particularly autobiographical
things.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:16.420
So you become very aware of who you are and
how you come across.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.910
But I don’t know?

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:30.920
I think of acting and stand-up as very different
disciplines but I also love acting because

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.190
in some ways it’s the opposite from stand-up.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:42.830
Whereas stand-up I’m writer, director, producer
and ever beat and moment of the show is controlled

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.150
and influenced by me.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:56.420
I love the opportunity to be this spoke in
this larger, just even a moment in a scene

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:04.030
serving a narrative of a story is really exciting
and also it’s fun to kind of hide in a different

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.959
character and have a different point of view.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:14.120
And I think in my stand up I kind of engage
different points of views.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:22.790
But in acting it’s really fun to kind fo
climb behind a different point of view and

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.489
see how they would adjust.

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:28.010
Of course, it’s still you.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:35.010
But like playing Paul Markham, you know, he’s
a lawyer.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.180
I think that influenced how he viewed everything.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.520
It was also somebody who really liked Ted
[Kennedy].

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.690
So I think those elements really had an influence.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:52.650
Whereas in stand-up there is a greater likelihood
of taking the irreverent point of view.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:56.269
Film Courage: Have we learned anything from
Chappaquiddick all of these years later?

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.989
Or it’s just a moment in time, no one really
knows what happened?

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:03.930
Jim Gaffigan: There’s so much information
out there.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:14.150
And some of it, the information I know know
how definite it is.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:20.590
I think it’s how our viewpoint on Chappaquiddick
is shifting, just particularly in this day

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:25.969
in age that we live in so…I mean look our
viewpoint on inebriated driving has shifted

00:04:25.969 --> 00:04:27.610
dramatically in our lifetime.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:33.110
I remember being a kid and hearing about inebriated
driving and being like “Try not to do it!”

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.219
And now, no one would do it.

00:04:35.219 --> 00:04:41.159
And so, obviously this tragedy, no one should
ever do this.

00:04:41.159 --> 00:04:49.370
But like the alcohol or the philandering part,
our judgments on that have changed dramatically.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:50.379
Film Courage: Excellent!

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:51.379
Thank you.

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:52.389
Jim Gaffigan: Thank you.

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:53.069
Appreciate it.

